[{:seon.task/name "one-documented-config"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `resources/seon/config/default.edn` holds every limit, bound and default in v1's shape (unit, provenance and bound beside each key), validated by `:seon.config/config` (a closed map, resources/seon/schemas/seon.config*.edn; v1's keys ported where relevant) when `seon.config` loads at boot. Readers: `#env` only where a script sets one; `#profile` by SEON_PROFILE (`:deepseek` for the paid model, `:scripted` for checks); `#seon/checkout` derives checkout paths at load from SEON_CHECKOUT (bin/env) or JOLT_PWD. The only `System/getenv` calls in src are `seon.config`'s checkout, profile and secret. Layers, later winning key by key and validated once: the shipped file, the checkout's `config/local.edn` (gitignored; bin/ports writes its ports), then `--config FILE` and `--set :ns/key=edn` from the command line; `seon.config/sources` and the `:seon.config/sources` fact name each key's layer. Root writes the config in force on main's root entity (`seon.config/facts`); `seon.config/from-db` reads it on main or a branch; the dashboard shows it by the schema's render (`seon.render.ui/config-panel`). No port is listened on unless the config says so (`:seon.config.web/port` and `:seon.config.root/server-port` ship 0). Octopus merges are off (`:seon.config.merge/max-running 1`). Not keys, because no clock decides anything (AGENTS §4): the agent stop bound (`seon.agent-proc/stop-bound-ms`) and the model's HTTP timeout are named defs in their owners (the stall silence is `:seon.config.agent/stall-ms` now: it decides nothing, only when a stall is recorded), left for `seon.root/shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow`, `seon.root/a-stall-is-past-the-requests-own-bound` and `seon.fx/a-transient-model-failure-parks`; checks pass the stall to `seon.root/start!` as an argument. Replaced: aero reading only system.edn, 16 config keys held as literals read from SEON_* at load, about 38 `System/getenv` calls with inline defaults in 14 files, and checkout paths baked at compile time (a worktree's binary reused next/'s path from the shared AOT cache)."
